183:
“Good friendship was praised by the Sage with reference to the world;
By associating with a good friend, even a fool may become wise.

One should associate with the virtuous; wisdom grows for those who associate;
By associating with the virtuous, one may be liberated from all sufferings.

One should know suffering, the origin of suffering, its cessation,
And the Eightfold Path—these four Noble Truths.

The Teacher, the charioteer of men to be tamed, has declared that the state of womanhood is painful;
Having co-wives is also painful; some women suffer even after giving birth once.

Some delicate women even cut their throats, others take poison;
Having fallen into the midst of a murderer, both (mother and child) experience ruinous calamities.

While I was traveling to give birth, I saw my dead husband;
Having given birth on the path, I had not yet reached my own home.

Two sons have died, and my husband died on the path, O miserable me!
My mother, father, and brother were burned on a single funeral pyre.

Bereft of family and miserable, you have experienced immeasurable suffering;
Your tears have flowed for many thousands of births.

I have lived in the midst of the charnel ground; I have seen the flesh of my sons being eaten;
With family destroyed, despised by all, and my husband dead, I have reached the deathless (Nibbāna).

I have developed the Noble Eightfold Path leading to the deathless;
Nibbāna is realized; I have looked into the mirror of Dhamma.

I have the dart removed, the burden laid down, and what was to be done is done;
The Elder Kisāgotamī, with a liberated mind, has spoken this.”

In that context, 'kalyāṇamittatā' means the state of having a good, excellent, and virtuous friend. One who establishes another in virtues such as morality (sīla), destroys suffering, and provides for welfare—such a person who assists in every way (is called a good friend).
